1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,676
In 1858, London smelled so bad... even
Parliament wanted to escape.

2
00:00:04,976 --> 00:00:09,435
Back then, the River Thames wasn't just a
river. It was London's giant open sewer.

3
00:00:09,535 --> 00:00:14,310
For decades, people dumped human waste,
dead animals, and industrial garbage
straight into it.

4
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:16,435
Then came the summer of 1858.

5
00:00:16,535 --> 00:00:21,545
Temperatures soared above 30 degrees
Celsius, and the filthy river began baking
in the heat.

6
00:00:21,745 --> 00:00:22,951
The smell was unbearable.

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,981
People couldn't stand near the river. And
inside Parliament?

8
00:00:26,081 --> 00:00:31,057
Politicians desperately closed the windows
and soaked the curtains in chemicals to
block the stench.

9
00:00:31,257 --> 00:00:32,084
It didn't work.

10
00:00:32,234 --> 00:00:34,160
There was even talk of moving Parliament.

11
00:00:34,410 --> 00:00:35,967
Finally, the government had enough.

12
00:00:36,067 --> 00:00:38,291
They rushed through a new law in just 18
days,

13
00:00:38,391 --> 00:00:43,426
and gave engineer Joseph Bazalgette the
green light to build a massive underground
sewer system.

14
00:00:43,676 --> 00:00:48,712
It took years to complete, but by 1875,
London's new sewer network was in place.

15
00:00:48,862 --> 00:00:51,004
The Great Stink didn't just embarrass
London.

16
00:00:51,104 --> 00:00:54,131
It forced an entire city to rebuild what
was underneath it.
